WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:06.760
Okay, so now let's have a quick look to the syntax of the stored procedure.

00:06.760 --> 00:07.720
It is very simple.

00:07.720 --> 00:09.280
So it has always two parts.

00:09.320 --> 00:11.880
First we have to define the stored procedure.

00:11.880 --> 00:12.960
So we can do it like this.

00:12.960 --> 00:13.920
Create procedure.

00:13.920 --> 00:16.280
Then we have to define the procedure name.

00:16.280 --> 00:19.120
And then we say as and then we have begin and end.

00:19.160 --> 00:23.400
It's very important for SQL to understand when the definition starts and when it ends.

00:23.440 --> 00:27.680
And then between the begin and end we're going to have a set of SQL statements.

00:27.680 --> 00:31.840
So here you can insert whatever you want, insert update queries anything.

00:31.880 --> 00:36.280
And once you have defined the stored procedure the next step is that we're going to go and execute it.

00:36.280 --> 00:37.480
So the syntax is very simple.

00:37.480 --> 00:38.680
We're going to say execute.

00:38.680 --> 00:40.240
And then the procedure name.

00:40.240 --> 00:45.800
So that's it with that SQL going to go to the third procedure and start executing all the SQL statements

00:45.800 --> 00:47.360
that you have in the definition.

00:47.360 --> 00:49.400
So this is the syntax of the stored procedure.

00:49.400 --> 00:50.800
As I said it is very simple.

00:50.840 --> 00:51.520
All right guys.

00:51.520 --> 00:53.120
So now let's do it step by step.

00:53.440 --> 00:55.760
The first step is that we're going to go and write a query.

00:55.760 --> 00:57.400
So let's say that we have a very simple task.

00:57.440 --> 01:03.690
And it says for us customers find the total number of customers and the average score.

01:03.730 --> 01:04.730
So let's go and do it.

01:04.810 --> 01:05.450
It's very simple.

01:05.450 --> 01:07.770
So select count star.

01:08.610 --> 01:10.330
Total customers.

01:10.650 --> 01:18.770
And then the average of scores as average score from our table sales customers.

01:19.010 --> 01:24.130
And then since it says US customers we have to go and filter the data based on the column country.

01:24.450 --> 01:26.130
Country is equal to USA.

01:26.250 --> 01:27.330
So that's it.

01:27.330 --> 01:28.170
This is our query.

01:28.210 --> 01:29.570
Let's go and execute it.

01:29.570 --> 01:34.330
So we have a very quick nice report about the total number of customers and the average score.

01:34.370 --> 01:40.010
So now let's say that I have a weekly meeting and I have to represent this report over and over.

01:40.050 --> 01:45.850
So that means I have to go and execute this query like frequently in weekly basis in order to get the

01:45.850 --> 01:47.290
data for the reports.

01:47.330 --> 01:51.610
So now what this means I have to go and save this query in order to use it later.

01:51.650 --> 01:53.530
Now that each time I have to rewrite it.

01:53.530 --> 01:59.490
So that means I have to store this text somewhere that I don't go and rewrite the query over and over.

01:59.730 --> 02:01.090
So what I usually do, let's go.

02:01.130 --> 02:05.370
And we copy the whole query, and then we create a new text.

02:05.410 --> 02:10.290
And let's say it's going to be my weekly query and it's going to be SQL.

02:10.410 --> 02:12.370
So I'm going to go and edit it.

02:12.370 --> 02:14.930
And here I'm going to save my query.

02:14.930 --> 02:19.810
And each time I need this query I have to go and copy it, go back to my SQL.

02:19.850 --> 02:22.970
And then I'm going to go and paste it in order to execute it.

02:22.970 --> 02:26.250
So either going to write it each time or copy and paste it.

02:26.290 --> 02:27.690
Well we don't have to do that.

02:27.690 --> 02:29.610
We have stored procedures.

02:29.610 --> 02:34.850
So that means we're going to go to the step two where we're going to turn this query into a stored procedure.

02:34.850 --> 02:35.650
So let's do that.

02:35.650 --> 02:36.530
It's very simple.

02:36.530 --> 02:39.530
So we're going to say create procedure.

02:39.690 --> 02:41.090
And now we have to go and give it a name.

02:41.090 --> 02:44.370
So it's going to be get customer summary.

02:44.370 --> 02:45.970
And then after that we're going to say us.

02:46.010 --> 02:48.490
And then we need the begin and end.

02:48.530 --> 02:50.930
And in between we're going to put our query.

02:50.930 --> 02:55.170
So let's go and copy our query and just put it in between.

02:55.170 --> 02:56.050
So that's it.

02:56.050 --> 02:57.850
Let's go and execute it.

02:57.890 --> 03:00.730
And with that we have created our stored procedure.

03:00.770 --> 03:05.660
And now in order to see our stored procedure we can go to the Object Explorer to our database sales

03:05.660 --> 03:06.060
DB.

03:06.100 --> 03:08.780
And then here we have a folder called programmability.

03:08.780 --> 03:10.140
So let's go inside it.

03:10.140 --> 03:12.940
And here we have a lot of stuff like functions triggers.

03:12.940 --> 03:14.500
And we have stored procedures.

03:14.500 --> 03:15.820
So let's go inside it.

03:15.820 --> 03:20.300
And we can see over here this is our new created stored procedure.

03:20.300 --> 03:21.660
So we are almost there.

03:21.700 --> 03:25.140
The next step is that we're going to go and call our stored procedure.

03:25.140 --> 03:26.740
And this is the easiest part.

03:26.780 --> 03:29.340
So it's going to be execute the stored procedure.

03:29.340 --> 03:30.620
And the syntax is very simple.

03:30.620 --> 03:31.700
So execute.

03:31.740 --> 03:33.860
And then the name of the stored procedure.

03:33.860 --> 03:36.900
So get customer summary.

03:36.900 --> 03:38.980
So let's go and execute it.

03:38.980 --> 03:42.220
And with that as you can see we get the result of our query.

03:42.220 --> 03:44.060
So as you can see it is very simple.

03:44.220 --> 03:47.300
In just few steps we created a stored procedure.

03:47.300 --> 03:49.860
And then in the future you don't need the whole thing.

03:49.980 --> 03:53.060
You just go and execute the stored procedure.

03:53.100 --> 03:58.380
I don't have to store the query locally at my PC or to copy and paste anything.

03:58.580 --> 04:04.260
If I want this reports now I just have to execute the stored procedure like this and I will get the

04:04.260 --> 04:04.780
results.
